First, we can expand the product:

\begin{align*}
(r - 1)(r + 2)(r + 6)(r + 3) &= [(r - 1)(r + 6)][(r + 2)(r + 3)] \\
&= (r^2 + 5r - 6)(r^2 + 5r + 6)
\end{align*}

Now, notice that the expressions inside the brackets look similar. This suggests a substitution. Let $s = r^2 + 5r$. Then, our expression becomes:

\begin{align*}
(r^2 + 5r - 6)(r^2 + 5r + 6) &= (s - 6)(s + 6) \\
&= s^2 - 36 \\
&= (r^2 + 5r)^2 - 36
\end{align*}

